> Which parts are you thinking of as "beankeeping"?

The restore ability changes are one example.  They're nice early on
when resources are scarce to have to deal with RA, but later on it's
just one more thing to stuff in the sack and carry around with you (or
another spellbook to track down).  Basically anything that increases
the amount of one-purpose-only equipment you need to tote around is
annoying (to me, anyway).

Magical eyes are sort of "player-beancounting"; you just blind
yourself and beat them senseless, so after the first couple of times
it's mostly an annoying "okay, now do a) b) c)" hoop to jump through--
but if there's other stuff around then blinding yourself is actually
meaningful.  If they only appeared in zoos and summon nasties (and
other places where they're contributing toward a larger problem) they
wouldn't annoy me.

Grey fungus: Yet another "go find some rocks and chuck them at it"
thing.  Having a few is interesting, and provides a challenge to new
players.  At least some of them provide some intrinsics.  But I'm not
sure we needed another.

MC changes: basically forcing the player to do some run away and
missile fire stuff more often.  Not something that's difficult to do
90% of the time, just vaguely annoying.  Having to go through this
well-known pattern (back off, throw missiles, back off, throw
missiles) that just takes a little longer than whack-whack-whack is't
more fun or skillful.  Again, if you get swamped then this change
suddenly becomes an actual challenge, so I don't really know what to
do about that.

It's essentially tied to the problem of complete resistances: having
those lets you introduce a challenge, but once the player's dealt with
it for a while and it's become monotonous, they can get protection
from it and move on to other challenges.  The problem with Vanilla is
that there aren't other challenges between mid-game and Rodney.  OTOH,
combinations of old challenges can present new scenarios for the
player--but it's hard to allow that payoff but not have the player
constantly dealing with solved problems.


